The macos-ax gate accepted an ATTESTATION that Accessibility was granted.

preflight.py --require-ax-permission gated on REPROIT_AX_PERMISSION_CONFIRMED=1,
an environment variable the workflow itself sets. It never asked macOS anything.
A runner whose grant was missing, revoked, or attached to a different binary
would pass preflight and fail later somewhere less obvious, which is the same
shape as every other defect this repository has paid for: a check that cannot
observe the thing it claims to check.

TCC makes this worse than a generic assumption. The grant is evaluated per
process AT LAUNCH and attributed to the responsible app bundle, so it can lapse
with nothing in the repository changing: rebuild or move the bundle, or restart
the service before the grant exists, and the attestation still reads "1".

Now the variable is the ACKNOWLEDGEMENT, deliberately kept because a machine
granting desktop control to CI should say so on purpose, and AXIsProcessTrusted()
is the EVIDENCE. Called through ctypes rather than pyobjc so the probe carries no
dependency of its own; a preflight that cannot run because its own import failed
would report the wrong thing.

CONTROLS, run locally:

  real probe here -> True
  CONTROL 1 ok: probe false, acknowledgement present -> refused
  CONTROL 2 ok: probe true, acknowledgement absent   -> refused
  CONTROL 3 ok: both present                         -> accepted

SETUP THIS PROVES OUT. This repository had zero self-hosted runners, so the gate
could never satisfy the required-CI rule Stable demands. Registered
reproit-ax-mac with labels self-hosted, macOS, ARM64, reproit-ax, matching the
job's runs-on exactly, as a user LaunchAgent rather than a system daemon because
Accessibility only works inside a logged-in GUI session.

The raw Runner.Listener could not be granted: it is an ad-hoc signed Mach-O and
the Privacy picker only offers things with a stable app identity, so it appeared
greyed out. It now runs inside ReproItAXRunner.app (identifier
dev.reproit.ax-runner, LSBackgroundOnly so it shows no window or Dock icon), and
the LaunchAgent was repointed at that bundle so the thing granted is the thing
that runs. Because the bundle is ad-hoc signed rather than Developer ID signed,
rebuilding or moving it can drop the grant; the failure then surfaces here, by
name, instead of silently.
